.TH NNGOBACK 1 "Release 6.3".UC 4.SH NAMEnngoback \- make news articles unread on a day-by-day basis..SH SYNOPSIS.B nngoback [ \-\fBh\fP ].I days.SH DESCRIPTION.I nngobackwill rewind the record file of \fBnn\fP(1) one or more days, i.e. markthe news articles which have arrived on the system during the last.I daysdays unread..LPIf you are not up-to-date with your news reading, you can also use\fBnngoback\fP to catch up to only have the last few days of newswaiting to be read in the following way:.br	nn \-a0.br	nngoback 3.brThe \fBnn\fP command will mark all articles in all groups as read (answer.I allto the catch-up question.)  The following \fBnngoback\fP will then makethe last three days of news unread again..LPExamples:.TPnngoback 0Mark the articles which have arrived today as unread..TPnngoback 1Mark the articles which have arrived yesterday and today as unread..TPnngoback 6Mark the articles which have arrived during the last week as unread..LP\fBnngoback\fP have two methods to rewind the rc file:.br\- scan the history file for the articles (option \-\fBh\fP), or.br\- use a copy of the active file with the `right' age (default)..LPNormally, \fInngoback\fP will run \fInntidy\fP on your rc file usinga copy of the active file saved on the requested day.  Using thismethod, you cannot go more than 14 days back..LPIf the \-\fBh\fP option is specified, the history file will be scannedto locate articles which have been received after the specified dayand mark those as unread in the rc file.  When this method is used,\fBnngoback\fP can be quite slow depending on the size of the historyfile in your news system; it uses \fBegrep\fP(1) to scan the file, soit must be read the entire file although the interesting data arefound towards the end of the file.  This method does not normally workwith NNTP or in a network environment..LPIt is a prerequisite that the script \fBback_act\fP is executed at anappropriate time once (and only once) every day.  Preferably this isdone by \fBcron\fP right before the bacth of news for `today' is received.\fBback_act\fP will maintain copies of the active file for the last 14days, which can be used directly to rewind the rc file..SH FILES.DT.ta \w'$lib/date_regexp'u+3m.\"ta 0 20~/.nn/rc	The record of read articles..br~/.nn/rc.bak1	The original rc file before goback..br$db/active.\fIN\fP	The \fIN\fP days `old' active file..br$lib/back_act	Script run by cron to keep the `old' active files..br$lib/date_regexp	Program to build an egrep(1) pattern..br$newslib/history	The list of all articles in the system..DT.LPThe \fIdate_regexp\fP program builds an egrep pattern to extract thehistory file entries for a specified number of days..LPOn some systems the history file is split into smaller files in asubdirectory called history.d; \fBnngoback\fP tries to locate theright history file to use..SH SEE ALSOnn(1), nncheck(1), nngrep(1), nntidy(1).brnnadmin(1M), nnquery(1M), nnusage(1M), nnmaster(8).SH NOTESThe `old' active files method also works with NNTP and with a shareddatabase (that is why the files are stored in the database directory). .LP\fBnngoback\fP does not check the age of the `old' active files; itwill blindly believe that active.0 was created today, and thatactive.7 is really seven days old!  Therefore, the \fIback_act\fPscript should be run once and only once every day for \fInngoback\fPto work properly..LPWhen the history file is used, days are known to split at 12 midnight.When old active files are used, days are counted relative to the timethe files were saved..SH AUTHORKim F. Storm, Texas Instruments A/S, Denmark.brE-mail: storm@texas.dk